          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My dad is 80, confused since this morning and has not passed water since yesterday. How urgent is this, and what would be looked for?",
          "answer": "**This needs medical assessment today, and sooner if he is getting worse.** Sudden confusion in an 80-year-old, together with no urine for about a day, should not wait for a routine appointment. Call your local emergency number or go to the nearest emergency department now. Do not wait to see if it passes. Call emergency services straight away if he is hard to wake, very drowsy, has trouble breathing, chest pain, weakness or numbness on one side, or a seizure.\n\nThe automated check I ran rated this as \"routine\" because none of its listed emergency patterns matched. That is not a clearance. The check only covers a fixed list of presentations, so it can miss serious causes.\n\nThe reference material I could search does not describe what clinicians check for this combination, so I can't tell you that from these sources. The urinary tract infection summary notes that confusion in older adults is often tied to other causes, and that a urine test alone does not establish a diagnosis. The ICD-10 coding summary lists disorientation and urinary retention as separate findings, which is why both matter here. A clinician will decide which tests are needed.\n\nSources: Urinary tract infection (General clinical reference); ICD-10-CM Chapter 18 (US clinical modification of ICD-10).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
